WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.080
Android storage is not one simple container.

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:16.020
Investigators may encounter internal shared storage, app-private storage, media directories, downloads, documents, caches, thumbnails, preferences, configuration files, and structured databases.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.920
Each area has a different meaning.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:27.660
A photo in a public media directory, a cache file created by an app, and a database record inside an app-private area should not be interpreted as the same kind of user action.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:38.040
App-private storage is especially important because many applications store settings, structured data, local messages, caches, account references, and activity records there.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:46.500
Access depends on device state, authorization, acquisition method, Android version, encryption, and tool capability.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:53.840
When app-private data is available, the analyst still has to understand which app created it and what the record means in that app's workflow.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.900
SQLite databases are common in Android analysis.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:06.600
Many apps use SQLite to store structured local data such as messages, contacts, browsing records, history, configuration, or cached content.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.020
The presence of a record is a starting point, not the conclusion.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:18.800
Analysts need to examine table names, fields, timestamps, deletion indicators, application behavior, and whether the record was generated locally or synchronized.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.660
Scoped storage and encrypted storage affect interpretation and acquisition.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:27.320
Scoped storage changes how apps access shared files.

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:33.940
Encryption protects stored data and can limit what is available depending on device state and lawful access conditions.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:42.160
These features are important because an incomplete acquisition is not necessarily a tool failure; it may reflect the security model and state of the device.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.220
File names, paths, and timestamps require care.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:53.480
A file path may reveal the application that created or cached a file, but it may not prove the user intentionally opened it.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.540
A thumbnail may indicate media processing, not direct viewing.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:04.640
A timestamp may reflect creation, modification, sync, copy, or database update behavior.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.700
Treat file system evidence as part of a broader artifact story.
